# .devcontainer/.env.local — Local DevContainer environment overrides
#
# Copy this file to .devcontainer/.env.local and uncomment the vars you want
# to override. The file is gitignored so your changes won't affect others or
# conflict with blueprint updates to devcontainer.json or .env.
#
# Two-layer override system (later layers win):
#   .devcontainer/.env       — project-level defaults, committed to the repo
#   .devcontainer/.env.local — your personal overrides (this file), gitignored
#
# Both files are sourced early in script/setup/bootstrap and script/setup/setup,
# so values set here take precedence over .env.
#
# ⚠ SCOPE LIMITATION: Variables here reach lifecycle hook scripts and interactive
# shell sessions only. They do NOT reach DevContainer features (image build time)
# or containerEnv. To change feature versions (Python, Node), edit devcontainer.json.
#
# After changing this file, run "Dev Containers: Rebuild Container" to apply.
#

# ── Home Assistant version ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Pin to a specific HA version instead of the one from hacs.json.
# Supported values:
#   "latest"        → newest stable release
#   "beta"          → newest pre-release (alpha/beta/RC)
#   "YEAR.MONTH"    → latest patch for that month  (e.g. "2026.4")
#   "YEAR.MONTH.N"  → exact stable version         (e.g. "2026.4.2")
#   "YEAR.MONTH.NbM"→ exact pre-release            (e.g. "2026.4.0b1")
# If unset, defaults to the version in hacs.json (resolved to latest patch).
#
# HA_VERSION=2026.4

# ── HACS installation ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set to "0" to skip HACS installation (speeds up setup if you don't need it).
# Default: "1" (install HACS)
#
# HA_INSTALL_HACS=0

# ── System package updates ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set to "1" to run apt-get update/upgrade during setup (useful in older images).
# Default: "0" (skip)
#
# APT_UPDATE=0

# ── Home Assistant API token (script/dev-api) ─────────────────────────────────
# A long-lived access token for the local development instance, used by
# ./script/dev-api to call service actions, read states, and send WebSocket
# commands against a running ./script/develop — which is how a derivation or a
# WebSocket-contract change gets checked against a live instance instead of only
# against the test suite.
#
# How to get one:
#   1. Start the instance:  ./script/develop
#   2. Open http://127.0.0.1:8123 and log in.
#   3. Click your user name (bottom of the sidebar) → "Security" tab.
#   4. Scroll to "Long-lived access tokens" → "Create Token", name it
#      something like "dev-api", and copy the value — it is shown once.
#   5. Paste it below, uncommented, and open a new shell (or run
#      `source .devcontainer/_load_env.sh` in the current one).
#
# The token belongs to the user who created it and inherits that user's rights,
# so create it with the dev account, not an admin account you also use elsewhere.
# It stays valid until you revoke it on that same Security page — revoke it there
# if it ever leaks, and mint a new one.
#
# ⚠ Keep this in .env.local, never in .env: .env.local is gitignored, .env is
# committed. This is also why ./script/dev-api reads HA_TOKEN and deliberately
# never reads config/.storage/auth — a token you granted on purpose is reviewable
# and revocable; harvesting the credential store is neither.
#
# Optional: point the scripts at a different instance (default is
# http://127.0.0.1:8123). Rarely needed inside the DevContainer.
#
# HA_TOKEN=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.PASTE_YOUR_OWN_TOKEN_HERE
# HA_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8123
